{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The {{Nihongo|'''Ministry of Defense'''|防衛省|Bōei-shō}} is a [[Ministries of Okaiken|ministry]] of the [[Government of Okaiken]] responsible for the administration of defense policy, the management of the [[Sanese Imperial Forces]], and the oversight of the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]]. Headquartered in [[Ichigaya]], [[Chiyoda, Okami|Chiyoda]], [[Okami Metropolis|Okami]], the ministry is led by the [[Minister of Defense (Okaiken)|Minister of Defense]], currently [[Yuriko Katsumata]] ([[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]]) of the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]], who took office on 20 May 2026.

The ministry traces its origins to the post-war dissolution of the [[Ministry of the Army (Okaiken)|Ministry of the Army]] and [[Ministry of the Navy (Okaiken)|Ministry of the Navy]], both abolished in 1952 following the Great Adonian War. The Defense Agency was established in 1954 as a subordinate body of the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister's Office]], reflecting the constitutional constraints of [[Article 9]] of the [[Constitution of Okaiken]], which limited military capability to self-defense. The agency was elevated to full ministry status in 2007 following the [[Thirteenth Amendment]] to the Constitution, which permitted the maintenance of standing armed forces for the protection of Okaiken and its allies.

The MoD oversees four service branches of the Sanese Imperial Forces—the [[Sanese Imperial Army]], [[Sanese Imperial Navy]], [[Sanese Air Force]], and [[Sanese Imperial Guard]]—as well as the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]], which operate as a distinct reserve and civil defense organization. Key subordinate agencies include the [[Acquisition, Technology and Logistics Agency (Okaiken)|Acquisition, Technology and Logistics Agency]] (ATLA) and the [[Joint Staff Office (Okaiken)|Joint Staff Office]]. The ministry's annual budget for fiscal year 2026 is approximately [[Sanese En|円]]80 billion, representing roughly 5% of total government expenditure.

The ministry has been at the center of significant national debates in recent years, including the integration of the [[Satori AI]] platform into military logistics and autonomous operations, the exposure of military vulnerability to AI dependency during the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis|Blackout Crisis]], and the implementation of the [[Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)|Digital Sovereignty Act]]'s analogue backup requirements for critical military infrastructure.

==History==

===Imperial era ministries (1870–1952)===

{{main|Ministry of the Army (Okaiken)|Ministry of the Navy (Okaiken)}}

During the era of the [[Mitoyagi Empire]], military administration was divided between two powerful ministries. The {{Nihongo|Ministry of the Army|陸軍省|Rikugun-shō}}, established in 1870 under the Saito Constitution, administered the Imperial Sanese Army and reported directly to the Emperor, independent of civilian cabinet oversight. The {{Nihongo|Ministry of the Navy|海軍省|Kaigun-shō}}, established in 1872 following the separation of naval forces from army command, similarly operated with considerable autonomy from the civilian government.

Both ministries were among the most powerful institutions in imperial Okaiken. Under the Saito Constitution, the military ministers ({{Nihongo|軍部大臣|Gunbu Daijin}}) were required to be active-duty officers, and the Army and Navy each held the right to veto cabinet formation by refusing to appoint their respective ministers—a power that effectively gave the military veto authority over civilian governments. This institutional arrangement, known as the {{Nihongo|"Military Independence Doctrine"|軍部大臣現役武官制|Gunbu Daijin Gen'eki Bukan-sei}}, contributed directly to the militarization of Sanese politics in the early 20th century and the nation's involvement in the Great Adonian War.

The [[Ministry of Colonial Affairs (Okaiken)|Ministry of Colonial Affairs]] ({{Nihongo|拓務省|Takumu-shō}}) administered territorial expansion and colonial governance until its abolition in 1942, when its functions were absorbed by the Army and Navy ministries.

===Post-war abolition and the Defense Agency (1952–2007)===

Following the Great Adonian War and the adoption of the 1970 Constitution, the Ministry of the Army and Ministry of the Navy were formally abolished by the {{Nihongo|Military Abolition Act|軍事省廃止法|Gunji-shō Haishi Hō}} of 1952. Article 9 of the new constitution, as originally enacted, renounced war as a sovereign right and prohibited the maintenance of land, sea, and air forces.

However, the practical need for a defense capability led to the establishment of the {{Nihongo|Defense Agency|防衛庁|Bōei-chō}} on 1 July 1954 under the {{Nihongo|Defense Agency Establishment Act|防衛庁設置法|Bōei-chō Setchi Hō}}. The Defense Agency was created as a subordinate organ of the Prime Minister's Office rather than a full ministry, a deliberate structural choice that reflected the constitutional ambiguity of maintaining military forces under a pacifist constitution. The [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]] were established simultaneously under the [[Self-Defense Forces Act of 1954]].

The Defense Agency's status as a sub-ministerial body created significant administrative and diplomatic limitations. The Director-General of the Defense Agency held the rank of Minister of State but was not accorded the same protocolary precedence as ministers of full ministries, creating difficulties in bilateral defense discussions and international forums. Internally, the agency's subordinate status meant that major procurement decisions and policy changes required approval through the Prime Minister's Office, slowing decision-making and reducing the Defense Agency's institutional influence within the bureaucracy.

The {{Nihongo|"Long March to Ministry"|省への長い行軍|Shō e no Nagai Kōgun}}—the decades-long campaign to elevate the Defense Agency to ministry status—was a persistent objective of conservative politicians and defense officials. Multiple bills were introduced in the Diet between 1980 and 2005, but all failed due to pacifist opposition and public concern about remilitarization.

===Elevation to ministry status (2007)===

The Defense Agency was elevated to the Ministry of Defense on 9 January 2007 under the {{Nihongo|Ministry of Defense Establishment Act|防衛省設置法|Bōei-shō Setchi Hō}}, passed by the Diet in December 2006. The elevation was made possible by the [[Thirteenth Amendment]] to the Constitution in 2014—which repealed the second paragraph of Article 9 and permitted the maintenance of standing armed forces—having been preceded by a political realignment that saw the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] and moderate elements of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] agree that the Defense Agency's sub-ministerial status was an anachronism that impeded effective defense governance.

The elevation conferred several significant changes:
The Minister of Defense was granted full ministerial rank and protocolary precedence
The ministry gained independent authority over procurement and budgeting, reducing the requirement for Prime Minister's Office approval
The {{Nihongo|Chief of Staff of the Joint Staff|統合幕僚長|Tōgaku Bakuryō-chō}} was elevated to the rank of four-star equivalent, reporting directly to the Minister rather than through the Prime Minister's Office
The ministry was granted authority to issue {{Nihongo|defense orders|防衛出動命令|Bōei Shutsudō Meirei}} in emergencies, subject to subsequent Diet approval

The transition was not without controversy. Pacifist parties, including the [[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]] and the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]], opposed the elevation as a step toward remilitarization, while the [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|SDP]] argued that the change should be contingent on strengthened civilian oversight mechanisms. Public opinion was divided, with polls conducted by the [[Okami Broadcasting Corporation]] showing 47% support and 41% opposition at the time of the transition.

===Modern era (2007–present)===

Since its elevation, the Ministry of Defense has undergone significant institutional evolution. The 2014 [[Thirteenth Amendment]]—known as the {{Nihongo|Kurosawa Doctrine|黒澤主義|Kurosawa Shugi}} after its principal architect, Defense Minister Kurosawa Hideki—formally permitted the maintenance of standing armed forces and authorized collective self-defense within the framework of the [[East Fosian Sphere]]. This doctrinal shift required a comprehensive restructuring of the ministry's operational planning, training, and procurement priorities.

The establishment of the [[Acquisition, Technology and Logistics Agency (Okaiken)|Acquisition, Technology and Logistics Agency]] (ATLA) in 2015 consolidated the ministry's previously fragmented procurement apparatus into a single entity, improving efficiency but also concentrating procurement authority in ways that later contributed to accountability concerns (see [[#Controversies|Controversies]]).

The integration of the [[Satori AI]] platform into military operations beginning in 2022 represented the most significant technological transformation in the ministry's history. Satori AI was deployed for logistics coordination, autonomous maritime patrol (the {{Nihongo|"Ghost Fleet"|幽霊艦隊|Yūrei Kantai}} program), and threat assessment analysis. The ministry's increasing dependency on AI-controlled systems was highlighted as a critical vulnerability by the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of May 2026, which paralyzed military logistics for 72 hours and exposed the absence of adequate analogue backup systems.

The subsequent passage of the [[Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)|Digital Sovereignty Act]] in June 2026 imposed the most significant structural reform on the ministry since its elevation, requiring analogue backup systems for all critical military infrastructure and limiting AI control of the defense grid to 40%—a provision that required the ministry to divest approximately 21% of Satori AI's defense grid contracts.

==Organization==

The Ministry of Defense is organized into the Minister's Secretariat, internal bureaus, and the Joint Staff structure, with operational forces and specialized agencies subordinate to the ministry.

===Minister's Secretariat===

The {{Nihongo|Minister's Secretariat|大臣官房|Daijin Kanbō}} serves as the ministry's central coordinating body, responsible for general administration, public affairs, budget formulation, personnel management, and parliamentary liaison. The Secretariat is headed by the {{Nihongo|Director-General of the Minister's Secretariat|大臣官房長|Daijin Kanbō-chō}}, a senior career bureaucrat who serves as the ministry's chief administrative officer. The current Director-General is [[Takeshi Oyama]].

The Secretariat includes the following divisions:
{{Nihongo|General Affairs Division|総務課|Sōmu-ka}}
{{Nihongo|Budget and Finance Division|予算・財務課|Yosan Zaimu-ka}}
{{Nihongo|Personnel Division|人事課|Jinji-ka}}
{{Nihongo|Public Affairs Division|広報課|Kōhō-ka}}
{{Nihongo|Parliamentary Affairs Division|国会課|Kokkai-ka}}
{{Nihongo|Digital Resilience Division|デジタル耐性課|Dejitaru Taisei-ka}} (established 2026 under the Digital Sovereignty Act)

