===Joint Staff===

{{main|Joint Staff Office (Okaiken)}}

The {{Nihongo|Joint Staff Office|統合幕僚監部|Tōgō Bakuryō Kanbu}} is the ministry's operational command structure, responsible for joint planning, coordination, and command of the Sanese Imperial Forces. It is headed by the {{Nihongo|Chief of Staff of the Joint Staff|統合幕僚長|Tōgō Bakuryō-chō}}, the highest-ranking military officer in the Sanese Imperial Forces, currently [[Genshiro Madokoro]].

The Joint Staff Office coordinates the operations of the four service branches through the Joint Operations Command and serves as the primary interface between the civilian leadership of the ministry and the uniformed services. The Chief of Staff reports directly to the Minister of Defense and, through the Minister, to the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]] as commander-in-chief.

==Agencies==

===Acquisition, Technology and Logistics Agency===

{{main|Acquisition, Technology and Logistics Agency (Okaiken)}}

The {{Nihongo|'''Acquisition, Technology and Logistics Agency'''|防衛装備庁|Bōei Sōbi-chō}}, commonly abbreviated '''ATLA''', is the ministry's principal procurement and research body. Established in 2015 through the merger of the Technical Research and Development Institute, the Procurement Office, and the Defense Facilities Administration Agency, ATLA consolidated the ministry's previously fragmented procurement apparatus into a single entity with an annual budget of approximately 円18 billion.

ATLA is responsible for:
Research, development, and procurement of defense equipment and systems
Management of defense technology cooperation with allied nations
Oversight of the domestic defense industrial base
Administration of the {{Nihongo|Defense Technology Strategy|防衛技術戦略|Bōei Gijutsu Senryaku}}, which guides long-term research priorities

ATLA has been the subject of significant controversy regarding procurement practices (see [[#Controversies|Controversies]]).

===Joint Staff Office===

{{main|Joint Staff Office (Okaiken)}}

The Joint Staff Office, described above, operates as a semi-autonomous agency within the ministry, with its own staff and support structure separate from the internal bureaus. It maintains the {{Nihongo|Joint Operations Center|統合作戦指揮所|Tōgō Sakusen Shikisho}}, the ministry's 24-hour operations monitoring facility at Ichigaya, which tracks potential threats and coordinates force responses.

===Defense Intelligence Headquarters===

The {{Nihongo|Defense Intelligence Headquarters|防衛情報本部|Bōei Jōhō Honbu}} is the ministry's intelligence analysis body, subordinate to the Intelligence and Security Bureau. It operates signals intelligence collection, imagery analysis, and open-source intelligence programs, and maintains liaison relationships with allied intelligence services through the [[East Fosian Sphere]] framework.

===Sanese Imperial Guard===

{{main|Sanese Imperial Guard}}

The {{Nihongo|Sanese Imperial Guard|近衛師団|Konoe Shidan}} is a distinct branch of the Sanese Imperial Forces with a dual role: it serves as the primary security force for the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]], the [[Imperial Palace]], and key government facilities in Okami, while also functioning as an elite rapid-response infantry unit. The Imperial Guard reports to the Minister of Defense through the Joint Staff Office but maintains a unique relationship with the Imperial Household, reflecting its historical role as the Emperor's personal guard.

==Defense policy and strategy==

===Industrial Sovereignty doctrine===

The {{Nihongo|'''Industrial Sovereignty''' doctrine|産業主義教義|Sangyō Shugi Kyōgi}} is the ministry's current strategic framework, adopted in 2024 under Defense Minister [[Masayoshi Kido]]. The doctrine holds that national defense capability is inseparable from domestic industrial capacity, and that reliance on foreign suppliers for critical defense technologies constitutes an unacceptable strategic vulnerability. The doctrine prioritizes:

'''Domestic production''' of critical defense platforms, including next-generation fighter aircraft, naval vessels, and missile systems
'''Technology sovereignty''' in AI, cyber, and electronic warfare capabilities
'''Supply chain resilience''' through diversification of suppliers and stockpiling of critical components
'''Analogue backup capability''' for all AI-dependent systems (reinforced by the Digital Sovereignty Act)

The Industrial Sovereignty doctrine replaced the previous {{Nihongo|"Dynamic Defense" framework|動的防衛枠組み|Dōteki Bōei Waku-gumi}}, which had emphasized rapid deployment capability and international interoperability since 2014. The shift was driven by growing concerns about supply chain vulnerability following global semiconductor shortages and the recognition that the ministry's increasing reliance on the Satori AI platform had created a single point of failure.

Minister Katsumata has endorsed the Industrial Sovereignty doctrine as consistent with the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]'s broader {{Nihongo|"Fortress State"|要塞国家|Yōsai Kokka}} philosophy, stating that "a nation that cannot manufacture its own defense systems cannot defend itself."

===Self-defense posture===

Under the current constitutional framework, as amended by the [[Thirteenth Amendment]] in 2014, the Sanese Imperial Forces are maintained "to protect Okaiken's most valuable assets" and the nation retains "the right to protect their assets and their foreign allies from threats of intimidation and war." This language, while broader than the original Article 9's strict pacifism, continues to impose significant constraints on the ministry's operational scope.

The ministry's interpretation of the right of collective self-defense, as established by the Kurosawa Doctrine, permits the Sanese Imperial Forces to come to the aid of allies under attack only when:
The attack threatens Okaiken's survival
There is no other means to repel the attack
The use of force is limited to the minimum necessary

This restrictive interpretation has been the subject of ongoing debate. The [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] has advocated for a broader interpretation that would permit proactive military operations in support of allies, while the [[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]] and [[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]] have called for a return to the original Article 9's strict pacifism.

===East Fosian Sphere commitments===

{{main|East Fosian Sphere}}

As a member of the [[East Fosian Sphere]], Okaiken maintains mutual defense obligations that require the ministry to maintain forces capable of contributing to collective defense operations. The ministry's force structure and procurement priorities are partially shaped by these commitments, which include:

Participation in joint exercises and training with Sphere member states
Maintenance of interoperable communications and command systems
Contribution to the Sphere's maritime patrol and surveillance framework
Hosting of Sphere coordination facilities at [[Tokuyama Naval Base]]

The East Fosian Sphere commitment has been a persistent source of domestic political tension, with pacifist parties arguing that collective defense obligations exceed the constitutional limits of self-defense, and conservative parties maintaining that the Sphere is essential to Okaiken's security in an increasingly contested region.

==Budget==

The Ministry of Defense's budget for fiscal year 2026 is approximately [[Sanese En|円]]80 billion, representing roughly 5% of total government expenditure (円1.6 trillion) and approximately 2.0% of GDP ($3.93 trillion). Defense spending has been gradually increasing since 2014, when the Thirteenth Amendment permitted the maintenance of standing forces, rising from approximately 円55 billion to the current level.

