The inclusion of a dedicated Digital Resilience and Analogue Backup budget line (円3 billion) is a direct consequence of the [[Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)|Digital Sovereignty Act]], which requires the ministry to establish and maintain analogue backup systems for all critical military infrastructure. This allocation, announced by Minister Katsumata in June 2026, represents the first dedicated funding for analogue military systems since the ministry's adoption of AI-dependent infrastructure.

==Key legislation==

The Ministry of Defense operates under a framework of interlocking statutes that define its authority, structure, and constraints:

'''{{Nihongo|Defense Agency Establishment Act|防衛庁設置法|Bōei-chō Setchi Hō}} (1954)''': Established the Defense Agency as a subordinate body of the Prime Minister's Office. Superseded by the Ministry of Defense Establishment Act but remains the foundational statute for the Self-Defense Forces.
'''{{Nihongo|Self-Defense Forces Act|自衛隊法|Jieitai Hō}} (1954)''': Established the Okaiken Self-Defense Forces and defined their mission, structure, and operational constraints. Amended extensively following the Thirteenth Amendment to reflect the expanded mandate of the Sanese Imperial Forces.
'''{{Nihongo|Ministry of Defense Establishment Act|防衛省設置法|Bōei-shō Setchi Hō}} (2006, effective 2007)''': Elevated the Defense Agency to full ministry status, conferring independent authority over procurement, budgeting, and operational command.
'''{{Nihongo|Thirteenth Amendment|第十三次改正|Dai-jūsan-ji Kaisei}} (2014)''': Repealed the second paragraph of Article 9 of the Constitution, permitting the maintenance of standing armed forces and authorizing collective self-defense. Known as the Kurosawa Doctrine.
'''{{Nihongo|ATLA Establishment Act|防衛装備庁設置法|Bōei Sōbi-chō Setchi Hō}} (2015)''': Established the Acquisition, Technology and Logistics Agency, consolidating the ministry's procurement apparatus.
'''[[Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)|Digital Sovereignty Act]] (2026)''': Requires analogue backup systems for all critical military infrastructure and limits AI control of the defense grid to 40%. The most significant structural reform to the ministry's technology governance since its establishment.

==Recent events==

===Satori AI military integration===

The Ministry of Defense began integrating the [[Satori AI]] platform into military operations in 2022, initially for logistics coordination and supply chain optimization. By 2025, Satori AI's military applications had expanded to include:

'''Logistics coordination''': AI-optimized supply chain management for all service branches
'''Threat assessment''': Real-time analysis of intelligence data for the Defense Intelligence Headquarters
'''Autonomous maritime patrol''': The {{Nihongo|"Ghost Fleet"|幽霊艦隊|Yūrei Kantai}} program, which deployed AI-controlled unmanned surface vessels for coastal surveillance and anti-submarine detection
'''Communication infrastructure''': AI-managed military communication networks and emergency alert systems
'''Fire control systems''': AI-assisted targeting and fire direction for coastal defense batteries

By May 2026, Satori AI controlled approximately 61% of the defense grid, making the ministry the platform's second-largest government client after the transportation sector. The ministry's internal risk assessments had identified AI dependency as a growing concern, but these warnings were not acted upon due to budget constraints and institutional pressure to modernize.

===Blackout Crisis impact===

{{main|Satori AI Blackout Crisis}}

The [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of 1–4 May 2026 had catastrophic consequences for military operations. When Satori AI's central neural processing cluster experienced a cascade failure, the following military systems were affected:

'''Logistics paralysis''': All AI-coordinated supply chain operations ceased, requiring manual inventory tracking and physical dispatch of supplies
'''Ghost Fleet shutdown''': The autonomous maritime patrol vessels lost central coordination and reverted to station-keeping mode, creating a 72-hour gap in coastal surveillance
'''Communication disruption''': AI-managed military communication networks failed, forcing reliance on backup radio systems that were insufficient for the volume of traffic
'''Fire control degradation''': AI-assisted coastal defense batteries lost targeting capability, requiring manual fire direction

The crisis exposed the complete absence of analogue backup systems for AI-dependent military infrastructure. The Joint Operations Center at Ichigaya was forced to coordinate defense operations using paper maps, physical message dispatch, and manual radio communication for 72 hours—an experience that the Chief of Staff later described as "the most professionally humiliating experience of my career."

A subsequent internal review, the {{Nihongo|Ichigaya After-Action Report|市ヶ谷事後検証報告|Ichigaya Jigo Kenshō Hōkoku}}, identified 14 critical systems that had no analogue backup and 8 systems where the analogue backup was connected to the same administrative interface as the primary AI system—rendering the backup equally vulnerable to cascade failure.

===Digital Sovereignty Act implications===

{{main|Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)}}

The [[Digital Sovereignty Act (Okaiken)|Digital Sovereignty Act]], passed in June 2026, imposed the most significant structural reform on the ministry since its elevation to ministry status. The Act's three core provisions— the 40% AI control cap, the analogue backup mandate, and the 72-hour Blackout Standard—each required major changes to the ministry's technology infrastructure:

'''40% AI control cap''': Required the ministry to reduce Satori AI's share of the defense grid from 61% to 40% within 12 months, necessitating the divestment of approximately 21% of Satori AI's defense contracts and the procurement of alternative AI systems or manual systems
'''Analogue backup mandate''': Required the establishment of physically isolated, human-operable backup systems for all critical military infrastructure, including logistics, communications, fire control, and surveillance
'''72-hour Blackout Standard''': Required all military systems to sustain essential operations for 72 hours without AI coordination, including on-site backup power, manual communication systems, and physical copies of essential operational data

Minister Katsumata established the {{Nihongo|Digital Resilience Division|デジタル耐性課|Dejitaru Taisei-ka}} within the Minister's Secretariat in June 2026 to coordinate compliance, and allocated 円3 billion in the FY2026 budget for analogue backup implementation. The ministry's compliance deadline is 8 July 2027.

==Controversies==

===The Kizan ATLA procurement scandal (2024–2025)===

The most significant controversy in the ministry's recent history involved the [[Acquisition, Technology and Logistics Agency (Okaiken)|ATLA]] and its relationship with [[Kizan Systems]], the developer of the Satori AI platform. The {{Nihongo|"Kizan ATLA Affair"|木讃調達事件|Kizan Chōtatsu Jiken}} came to public attention in November 2024 when the [[Board of Audit of Okaiken|Board of Audit]] published a report revealing that ATLA had awarded 円12.7 billion in defense procurement contracts to Kizan Systems between 2022 and 2024 through a {{Nihongo|"simplified bidding process"|簡易入札手続|Kan'ei Nyūsatsu Tetsuzuki}}—a procedure intended for low-value, routine purchases that bypasses the standard competitive tender requirements.

The Board of Audit found that:
ATLA had structured 23 separate contract awards to Kizan Systems so that each fell below the 円600 million threshold that would trigger full competitive bidding
The contracts were for components of a single integrated system—the Satori AI military integration—making the use of simplified bidding a circumvention of procurement law
ATLA officials had received 47 "courtesy briefings" from Kizan Systems lobbyists during the procurement period, including 12 briefings held at Kizan's corporate hospitality suite at the [[Okami Imperial Hotel]]
A senior ATLA official, {{Nihongo|Hiroshi Matsunaga|松永浩|Matsunaga Hiroshi}}, had accepted a position on Kizan Systems' advisory board three months after retiring from ATLA, in violation of the {{Nihongo|Revolving Door Prevention Act|公務員退職後就職制限法|Kōmuin Taishokugo Shūshoku Seigen Hō}}

The scandal led to the resignation of ATLA Commissioner {{Nihongo|Kenji Furuya|古屋健二|Furuya Kenji}} in January 2025 and a criminal investigation by the [[Tokuyama District Public Prosecutors Office]]. The [[Competitive Bidding Reform Act of 2024]], passed in response to the scandal, introduced mandatory public tender requirements for all defense contracts above 円300 million and strengthened the revolving door restrictions for procurement officials.

The Kizan ATLA Affair was cited extensively during the Diet debate on the Digital Sovereignty Act as evidence that the ministry's procurement practices had enabled the concentration of AI control in a single company, creating the conditions for the Blackout Crisis.

===Civilian-military tensions===

The ministry has experienced recurring tensions between civilian leadership and the uniformed services, a structural issue inherited from the imperial-era military independence doctrine. The most significant recent incident occurred in 2023, when the Chief of Staff of the Joint Staff at the time, {{Nihongo|General Isamu Kurokawa|黒川勇|Kurokawa Isamu}}, publicly contradicted Defense Minister Kido's statement that the Ghost Fleet program was "experimental and non-operational" by telling the [[House of Officers]] Defense Committee that the autonomous vessels had been conducting operational patrols since October 2022.

General Kurokawa's testimony, which was widely reported as a "civilian-military split" by the [[Okami Shimbun]], led to his early retirement and prompted a review of the ministry's protocols for military testimony before the Diet. The incident highlighted the ongoing tension between the civilian leadership's desire to manage public perception of AI integration and the military's expectation of operational transparency.

